<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<chapter xml:id="chap-measure-space" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>Measure Spaces</title>
<introduction>
<blockquote>
<p><q>It is frequently claimed that Lebesgue integration is as easy to teach as Riemann integration. This is probably true, but I have yet to be convinced that it is as easy to learn.</q></p>
<attribution>
T.W. Körner
</attribution>
</blockquote>

<p>
    In this chapter we introduce our main objects of study: measures and measure spaces.
    We then look at some key properties of these objects.
    In <xref ref="sec-construction"/> we prove the Carathéodory-Hahn extension theorem. 
    This allows us to construct measures from simpler functions.
    The Carathéodory-Hahn extension theorem is the primary tool we will use to construct measures.<fn>at least until <xref ref="chap-dual-spaces"/>, when we prove the Riesz-Markov-Kakutani representation theorem; another tool for constructing measures.</fn>
    In <xref ref="chap-lebesgue-measure"/> we use the Carathéodory-Hahn extension theorem to construct measures on the real line.
    In particular we will construct the Lebesgue measure in <xref ref="chap-lebesgue-measure"/>, the first example of a measure introduced, and the motivating example for much of the theory.
</p>
</introduction>

<xi:include href="./2-measure-spaces-1.ptx" />
